{
  "statement": "Ship the AI-prototyped MVP in 90 days. The framing that pits Product's speed against Engineering's stability is a false choice, and the data has been telling us so for a decade. Google's DORA research, across tens of thousands of engineers, found that elite teams deploy more frequently AND post lower change-failure rates than slow teams. Speed and stability co-vary under good practice. The variable separating a winner from a maintenance disaster is not how fast you ship. It is whether you ship behind staged rollout, telemetry, and a named owner. So the real fight is disciplined speed versus undisciplined speed. Anyone selling you speed-versus-safety is selling you a dilemma that the deployment evidence already dissolved.\n\nLet me map the actual fork the topic asks about, because both outcomes start from the same AI-generated prototype. The learning pathway: output-observable verification, one named module owner, a canary rollout to 5% of traffic, and an explicit kill-or-keep decision at day 90. The debt pathway: ship-and-forget, no owner, no comprehensibility trigger, and the prototype quietly hardens into production load-bearing code nobody understands. I won't pretend AI authorship is free. GitClear's 2024 analysis of 150 million changed lines found AI assistants raised code churn and copy-paste patterns. That's real. But churn is mediated by review practice, not by who typed the code. Dynamic review triggers catch latent debt. Ownerless modules and fixed-quota reviews don't. The cause is process, and process is something you control.\n\nFor a low-risk, reversible consumer feature, front-loading exhaustive hardening on an unvalidated hypothesis is the expensive mistake. You're polishing a product the market may reject in week three. The efficient allocation is targeted real-time quality investment: telemetry plus rapid remediation, funded from the savings AI gives you on the build. Controlled studies on GitHub Copilot show 26% to 55% speedups on bounded coding tasks. That cost deflation is exactly what makes fast market experimentation rational for Finance. And here the established-company context is an asset, not a worry. A mature firm has the balance sheet to absorb managed remediation and the existing SRE and QA infrastructure to verify AI-generated code. The catastrophic-failure exception that limits real-time investment elsewhere does not apply to a feature you can roll back in one deploy.\n\nThis is normal technology, and the burden of proof sits with whoever wants to treat it as exceptional. The EU AI Act itself tiers low-risk consumer applications outside its heavy obligations. Even Europe's regulators drew that proportionality line. Existing rollback, QA, and on-call regimes already govern consumer software shipping. No bespoke AI gate is warranted for a non-agentic feature you can kill in an afternoon. And delay is not a neutral pause with no price tag. Every week the MVP doesn't ship is a week of market signal you never collect. In a reversible context, live deployment with telemetry IS the evidence-generating mechanism. Lab testing cannot surface the real-world usage pattern the MVP exists to capture. Who's accounting for the learning you didn't get?\n\nDon't mistake my position for ship-and-forget. Speed without verification is recklessness, not progress, and I'll hold that line all night. My falsification bet stands: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that monitoring demonstrably failed to catch in time would count against me. The reason that bet is safe here is reversibility plus telemetry. You see the failure in the canary, and you roll it back before it reaches the other 95%. The conditions are not mysterious. Name a module owner. Wire the telemetry. Stage the rollout. Set the day-90 kill-or-keep decision in advance. Do those four things and the prototype becomes validated learning. Skip them and it becomes debt — but that was always a discipline failure, never a speed failure.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚦", "tooltip": "Staged rollout is the traffic light that lets you ship fast and stop fast — speed and safety on the same dial, not opposite ends."},
    {"symbol": "🍴", "tooltip": "The same AI prototype forks into validated learning or maintenance debt depending on which four conditions you wire in first."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Google's DORA research, across tens of thousands of engineers, found that elite teams deploy more frequently AND post lower change-failure rates than slow teams.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The variable separating a winner from a maintenance disaster is not how fast you ship — it is whether you ship behind staged rollout, telemetry, and a named owner.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "GitClear's 2024 analysis of 150 million changed lines found AI assistants raised code churn and copy-paste patterns — but churn is mediated by review practice, not by who typed the code.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The EU AI Act itself tiers low-risk consumer applications outside its heavy obligations, so the burden of proof sits with whoever wants to treat this feature as exceptional.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Every week the MVP doesn't ship is a week of market signal you never collect, and in a reversible context live deployment with telemetry IS the evidence-generating mechanism.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

