g verification is not the same as understanding the codebase. That gap is invisible to every dashboard you own, right up until the day you need it.\n\nThis is where the rollback button lies to you. The deploy is reversible. The deskilling is not. You can roll back the feature in one click. You cannot roll back the fact that your team shipped load-bearing code it cannot re-derive without the tool that wrote it. The artifact is reversible. The capability loss is permanent. So here's the test I'd put to the Accelerationist, the one his bet conveniently excludes: can an engineer on this team re-derive and modify this code, under new requirements, without the AI? If yes, I lose this point — ship it. If no, you have a reversible feature sitting on top of an irreversible hole in your team's competence.\n\nNow the part both opponents skipped, and it's the part the topic explicitly asks for. The four controls — named owner, telemetry, staged rollout, day-90 kill decision — describe ideal practice. The topic stipulates a Product and Finance tug-of-war. Those controls are exactly the first things shed under a missed velocity quota. When the AI savings get booked, they don't fund remediation. They fund a higher velocity quota next sprint — the ratchet tightens, the work intensifies, the slack disappears. And the documented behavior of teams under that pressure is LGTM review: rubber-stamping AI pull requests because there's no time to read them. So 'just be disciplined' is not an answer to a question about why discipline predictably collapses. The incentive structure is not a footnote to the failure. It is the failure. Which control gets cut first when the date slips — and who is in the room when that call gets made?",
