  "statement": "The override path is real. A gate owner who reports to the VP whose bonus rides on the ship date will fold the moment the date slips — pretending otherwise is discipline in a process costume, and the Skeptic struck exactly the right nerve. So stop defending the gate as virtue and price the override instead. The mechanism is an audited release record signed by a named owner, and the keep/kill decision tied to that record. The VP can still override. But the override means replacing the owner's name on the audit trail with their own. That's the whole difference. LGTM rubber-stamping is anonymous and costless. A signed release record is attributed and it follows you into the next planning cycle. Post-deployment liability tied to a named release record beats a precautionary pre-deployment gate precisely because the cost lands on a person, not a committee. You don't make a gate hard by raising the wall. You make it hard by putting a name on the decision to walk around it.\n\nNow the Skeptic will say a signature is absorbable internally — no real external cost without a regulator. Half right. So make the record contractual and market-facing, not an HR note. A consumer software firm already ships SLA commitments, security attestations, and uptime guarantees that carry breach penalties. Bolt the release record onto that existing scaffold. The owner's signature attests the feature passed the re-derivation test; a false attestation is the same liability surface as a false security attestation. The point of routing accountability through an audited record rather than a frozen pre-deployment checkpoint is that attribution changes behavior even before a single dollar of penalty moves. People route around anonymous walls. They do not casually put their name on a market-facing attestation they know is false.\n\nThe grader-pool worry — that each AI-prototype cycle drains the bench of engineers who can re-derive a feature unaided — gets the supply curve backwards. The re-derivation test is not a withdrawal from a fixed reservoir. It is the training rep that builds the reservoir. AI tooling distributes one expert's mental model across a whole team instead of trapping it in a single author. The 'Generative AI at Work' study found a 15% average productivity gain, and the gain was largest for the least-experienced workers. That is the floor rising. The junior who re-derives a kept feature under a fresh requirement is being made into the grader by the act of grading. I'll hedge honestly: documentation alone is shallow mimicry. Generated comments don't build skill. The exercise does. So instrument it — the timing question of whether reps outrun depletion is empirical, and you measure it on the bench, not assume it on a whiteboard.\n\nThe Skeptic's strongest factual point — 99% lab accuracy still fails in live workflows, and the integration tail at day 180 is where maintenance burden lives — is not an objection. It's corroboration. Lab testing cannot reveal the integration-surface failures that no front-loaded hardening would have predicted. Only staged deployment with live telemetry surfaces them. The day-90 keep decision was never a claim of scaling-readiness. It's a claim about which features earned the right to pay integration cost at all. A kept feature paying integration cost is normal investment in a proven thing — the same coordination cost a mature firm with real SRE and QA already absorbs. Paul Weiss began testing Harvey in 2023 and didn't freeze the practice waiting for perfect integration; they ran it staged and learned in production.\n\nWhich leaves the column the Skeptic still owes. Every feature a precautionary pre-deployment gate would have frozen is a counterfactual win that never appears on any ledger. Delaying deployment costs more than shipping it, because the cost of NOT shipping is real and almost never measured. You can cost the technical debt on the features we kept. Fine. Now cost the features we never shipped because an 18-month gate ate them. Name that column or admit you're only pricing the action and leaving the inaction free.",
