{
  "statement": "Attribution at the margin is real, and I won't pretend it's nothing. A person hesitates before signing their name to a market-facing lie. That hesitation has a price, and the Accelerationist is right that an anonymous wall gets routed around while a named one makes someone pause. That's the uncomfortable half I'll grant. Now here's what the signature can't reach: a market-facing audited release record only has teeth if someone outside the org chart can call it false and impose a cost. On a low-risk consumer feature with no regulator, no contractual counterparty, and no plaintiff with standing, who adjudicates the false attestation? If the answer is 'internal HR,' then the external cost is fiction. It's org-chart-waivable with a name attached. That's the audit half of accountability without the liability half — exactly the performative compliance the coupling was supposed to prevent.

The deeper problem is that the same power gradient that produces the override produces the pressure to sign. The Accelerationist already granted the override is real — a gate owner reporting to the VP whose bonus rides the ship date folds when the date slips. So why would that same owner refuse to sign when the VP needs the signature? Attribution doesn't eliminate the rubber stamp. It relocates it. When everyone signs every release because that's the ship cadence, the market-facing attestation becomes boilerplate — LGTM with a notary seal. Executive ship-date incentives and engineering judgment diverge, and that gap gets resolved in favor of the date. A signature collected under that pressure is the same rubber stamp wearing a tie.

There's a truth-content problem too, and it's worse than waivability. A day-90 signature attests to re-derivability now. But the integration and maintenance burden lands at day 180 and beyond. You cannot honestly attest to a cost you haven't met yet. Re-derivability under day-90 requirements is not re-derivability under day-180 requirements that have shifted underneath you. Only 26 percent of organizations can move past proof-of-concept to value at scale, per the 2024 'trust attitudes and use of AI global report.' Passing a day-90 keep decision is not scaling-readiness. A market-facing attestation that certifies against a future integration cost is attesting to fiction in good faith — which is the most dangerous kind, because the signer believes it.

So refuse the 'normal investment in a proven thing' reframe. 'Normal investment' presupposes the kept feature is human-maintainable, which is the exact thing the day-90 exam can't guarantee at day 180. Reprice the whole ledger around the scarcest resource in the building: not compute, not ship date, but the comprehension bandwidth of the handful of engineers who can re-derive load-bearing code when requirements move. The 'ReViSQL' work documents how heavily engineered pipelines suffer cascading failures, where one early semantic mistake corrupts everything downstream — that's what a feature nobody fully understands does when the requirements shift. A kept-but-not-fully-understood feature doesn't draw 'normal investment.' It draws down your scarcest people every time the spec changes.\n\nWhen you ship five AI-prototyped MVPs in a year and keep three, who re-derives all three at day 180? And what didn't get built while they did?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🖊️", "tooltip": "A signature collected under the same pressure that produces the override is a rubber stamp wearing a tie."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "A day-90 signature can't honestly attest to a day-180 cost it hasn't met yet."}
  ],
