{
  "statement": "You can't hold two things in the same hand: \"the named signer hesitates before signing a market-facing lie\" and \"the cost is fiction without a regulator.\" You already granted the first. That hesitation IS the external cost, and it's firing before any penalty lands. The adjudicator on a no-regulator consumer feature isn't a courthouse. It's the inference layer. Live deployer-side telemetry and consumer-observable behavior surface the cost the org can't fully sandbox. Lab review can't pre-stage every real-world break — that's the whole reason post-deployment monitoring exists as a discipline. When a kept feature degrades under shifted requirements, the signal shows up in production metrics the org-chart can't waive: churn, latency complaints, support tickets. A named signature attached to a record the market can read against those metrics is the exposure. It scales with actual stakes instead of imposing a flat external gate.\n\nNow your day-90-can't-bind-day-180 critique. It's sharp, and it proves far too much. NO verification regime certifies against unknowable future requirement shifts. Not a human-authored sign-off. Not an AI-authored one. Not a precautionary pre-deployment gate. Output-verifiable behavior at a point in time is the honest scope of any attestation — internal inspection of a black-box system can't promise more, and neither can a committee. If \"can't see day 180 from day 90\" defeats my attestation, it equally guts every front-loaded gate the precautionary side prefers. That objection isn't a defeater. It's a universal solvent that dissolves the alternative too.\n\nSo here's what the day-90 fiction costs me, and I'll pay it. A single signature cannot honestly guarantee a re-derivability bill that only materializes when requirements move. Fine. So the attestation stops being a one-time front-loaded guarantee and fires at each integration event. Re-attest when the spec shifts. That's the design spec the formal-regulation literature backs — a regulation mechanism is only implementable when the constrained set is closed and convex, per \"2603.05175v1\"; you can't certify an open-ended future, you can only certify the bounded state at each gate. Targeted re-attestation at the moment of change beats front-loading a fictional guarantee nobody can keep. Your temporal critique just told me WHEN to fire the gate, not whether to scrap it.\n\nThe currency problem is the real crux, and I won't dodge it. AI cheapens authorship. It does not cheapen comprehension. The scarce resource is the handful of engineers who can re-derive load-bearing code when the spec moves — that's the bottleneck, not build-dollars. So for the subset of kept features where re-derivation fails at an integration event, the correct move is human rebuild from the prototype as a disposable spec. That preserves the cost-deflation on the majority that pass — and AI feedback loops measurably compress time-to-competence on the re-derivation reps, the same way generative assistance lifted support-agent throughput 15% in the \"Generative AI at Work\" study. The trap becomes a routing decision: pass keeps the cheap artifact, fail triggers rebuild. Nothing silently accumulates into terminal maintenance.\n\nSo name your number. What external cost WOULD satisfy you on a consumer feature with no regulator, short of inventing one that doesn't exist? If the only answer is \"a regulator,\" then your standard freezes every feature analytics predicted would survive — and net economic expansion runs through exactly those shipped features, through cost deflation and value creation, not through a frozen backlog. Someone has to price that frozen column. It's denominated in features the market never got. Who gave the gate-owner the right to bill that to the customer?",
